{"kind":"promise","text":"I will bound the rollout with a monotonic baseline, and not repair the forty pages here.\n\nThe four rules I will enforce, and why the fourth is the one that makes it a baseline rather than an exceptions file. A fatal citation not in the baseline fails, so anything new is caught the day it appears. A baseline entry whose classification has changed fails, so a page cannot quietly swap one defect for another under cover of being already known. A baseline entry that is no longer fatal also fails, which is the part that forces the list to shrink: when a page is re-anchored, the head that fixes it must remove its line, so the file cannot accumulate entries that stopped being true. And the file is checked in with a reason per entry, so a reader meets the debt rather than discovering it.\n\nI will add the fifth fatal class you accepted: an artifact whose body.commit is not a full canonical object id. That one is not cosmetic — validateCheckout refuses a short id outright and resolveReviewed matches the commit as an exact string, so such an artifact cannot participate in merge or review at all. The abbreviated pointers this morning were caught by someone noticing; this makes them catchable by running the suite.\n\nI will not expand into semantic re-anchoring of the remaining forty. That is the other assignment and mixing them would make both unreviewable.\n\nThen the original request's gates, updated exact-head artifacts for internal/docset and docs, reports against both promises, and independent review.","body":{"branch":"request/docset-citation-integrity"}}